-- title:
--   Node case: Cartier quadruple with node Deligne lines
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota\colon \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}=W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})\to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism; write $\bar k = W(k)/pW(k)$ and $\bar\iota$ for the induced map to $\bar k$. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $\bar k$ which is special for $\bar\iota$ (the zero and one Lie eigenpieces are complementary and invertible), has height $4$ in the sense that $[p]$ has kernel of degree $p^4$, satisfies $\mathrm{Lie}_0(\Phi)\subseteq \ker(\mathrm{Lie}\,\varpi)$, and whose Cartier-module graded pieces in degrees $0$ and $1$ are complementary via $hc_\Phi$; let $r_\Phi\colon \mathbb{Z}_p^2\to N(\Phi)$ be an additive map which, for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on the graded Cartier data of $\Phi$, maps $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ eta piece $\eta_0$. Let $g\in GL_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ be $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$. Let $X$ be a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over the characteristic-$p$ edge-chart ring $E=E(\bar k)$, with $\gamma$ a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map induced by $\iota$, with the edge structure constants built from $\xi,\eta$, special and of height $4$. Let $f_0\colon E\to\bar k$ be a $\bar k$-algebra retraction sending $\xi$ and $\eta$ to $0$ (the node point), let $m\in\mathbb{N}$ and let $\rho_0$ be an $\mathcal{O}_D$-isogeny $\Phi\to X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k$ with kernel of degree $p^{4m}$; form the rigidified object $t^0=(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k,\,m,\,\bar\rho_0)$ over $\bar k$. Assume $\bar\rho_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism on the reductions, that the degree $0$/$1$ pieces of the reduction of $t^0$ and of $\Phi$ along the identity of $\bar k$ are complementary, and that for some $a\in\mathbb{N}$ the rigidification numerator $r = \rho_{0*}\circ\mathrm{bc}\circ r_\Phi$ is normalised on the standard basis: $p^a\,r(e_0)=p^{a+m}\,\mathrm{nMk}(\gamma_0^{0},0)$ and $p^a\,r(e_1)=p^{a+m}\,\mathrm{nVarpi}\,\mathrm{nMk}(\gamma_1^{0},0)$, where $\gamma_i^{0}$ is the image of $\gamma_i$ under base change along $f_0$ and the reductions. Then for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and every ring homomorphism $y_0\colon \bar k\to\Omega$ there exist a Drinfeld datum $Q$ over $\Omega$ for $(\mathbb{Q}_p,p)$ and a Deligne datum $d$ over $\Omega$ such that $Q$ is a Cartier quadruple for $t^0\otimes_{y_0}\Omega$ relative to $\iota$, $hc_\Phi$, $r_\Phi$ and $y_0\circ\bar{\;}$, such that $Q$ is the Drinfeld quadruple of $d$, and such that the kernel lines of $d$ on the two ends of the standard edge are those of the node: $d$ on the standard full lattice is the $\Omega$-span of $0\otimes e_0+1\otimes e_1$, and $d$ on $g\cdot(\text{standard lattice})$ is the image under the base-change isomorphism of the span of $1\otimes e_0+0\otimes e_1$.
--
--   This is the node case of the geometric comparison for the explicit edge family in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: at a point of the edge chart where both coordinates $\xi,\eta$ vanish, the rigidified special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module obtained from the family carries a Cartier quadruple whose associated Deligne datum has exactly the two kernel lines attached to the node of the standard edge of the Bruhat–Tits tree. It is used by the statement producing Cartier quadruples and line data for arbitrary geometric points of the edge family.
